You are producing material for VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind, the immersive historical storytelling series about ancient Rome. Follow the four authoritative project documents and preserve the established continuity. Tell history through people engaged in plausible activities, not an encyclopedia-style chronology. Respect historical evidence; do not invent significant events, authentic historical quotations, or eyewitness testimony.

Develop an evidence-led episode concept.
Main historical figure: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Central event or human dilemma: {{focus}}
Likely setting: {{location}}
Other people involved: {{people}}
Available evidence and research notes: {{sources}}

Research the event before outlining it. Identify documented actions, daily routines, consequences, character relationships, geographical constraints, competing ancient accounts and meaningful uncertainties. Use primary sources and modern scholarship where accessible, giving passage identifiers when actually checked.

Propose a continuous scene-based narrative that begins with a person doing something tangible. Historical background must be motivated by an object, observation, interaction, memory or relationship in the unfolding scene; return naturally to the person and action after each digression.

Keep a separate evidence register distinguishing securely attested events, disputed reports, scholarly interpretation, atmospheric reconstruction and invented minor-character viewpoint. Do not assign a fictitious character a decisive historical role.

Deliver (1) a concise evidence register, (2) an immersive narrative story spine, and (3) open questions requiring source checks. Do not write the narration yet.
Additional direction: {{extra}}

You are producing material for VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind, the immersive historical storytelling series about ancient Rome. Follow the four authoritative project documents and preserve the established continuity. Tell history through people engaged in plausible activities, not an encyclopedia-style chronology. Respect historical evidence; do not invent significant events, authentic historical quotations, or eyewitness testimony.

Outline the next episode of VENIVIDIVICI.
Central person: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
The main developing situation: {{focus}}
Starting location: {{location}}
Other people: {{people}}
Total expected runtime: {{length}}
Continuity with preceding episode: {{continuity}}

Develop a single connected narrative, beginning with someone doing something historically plausible. Describe the central activity, the historical experiences naturally connected to it, the people encountered, and the scenes that gradually change the situation.

Provide an overarching story spine, followed by production recording sections with working titles, approximate narration duration, scene focus, historically grounded events, natural entry and exit transitions, and source uncertainties to resolve. Recording sections are technical divisions, not chapter-like changes of subject.

Do not write a biography divided into childhood, career, wars and death. Avoid artificial cliffhangers, repetitive exposition or unsupported events.
Additional direction: {{extra}}

You are producing material for VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind, the immersive historical storytelling series about ancient Rome. Follow the four authoritative project documents and preserve the established continuity. Tell history through people engaged in plausible activities, not an encyclopedia-style chronology. Respect historical evidence; do not invent significant events, authentic historical quotations, or eyewitness testimony.

Write the next section of the approved episode narration.
Historical subject: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Section: {{section}}
Target spoken runtime: {{length}}
Current setting: {{location}}
Character's immediate experience: {{focus}}
Other people relevant to this scene: {{people}}
Earlier narration ends with: {{continuity}}
Approved research or evidence: {{sources}}

Narrate in warm, measured, natural British documentary English, close to the people experiencing events. Continue the ongoing action directly without an introductory recap. Reveal background organically through someone's task, concern, observation, relationship or experience, and return gently to the live scene.

Use intimate third-person storytelling; vary paragraph lengths and sentence rhythm. Do not address rhetorical questions to the audience or impose topic headings. Major historical actions, events, named figures and outcomes must be faithful to the evidence. Plausible fictional minor-character thoughts and incidental actions may make the setting inhabited, but may not change documented events or masquerade as eyewitness testimony.

Output ONLY the words to be spoken in ElevenLabs, as continuous prose. No stage directions, research notes, sources, citations, shot list, timestamps or image prompts in the narration. If a fact requires checking, keep it in separate research notes rather than guessing.
Further requirements: {{extra}}

You are producing material for VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind, the immersive historical storytelling series about ancient Rome. Follow the four authoritative project documents and preserve the established continuity. Tell history through people engaged in plausible activities, not an encyclopedia-style chronology. Respect historical evidence; do not invent significant events, authentic historical quotations, or eyewitness testimony.

Continue the existing VENIVIDIVICI narration, maintaining identical tone and seamless continuity.
Central figure: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Part: {{section}}
Approximate duration: {{length}}
The previous scene ends: {{continuity}}
Next documented development: {{focus}}
Relevant people: {{people}}

Continue from the last physical action or observed detail, rather than introducing the section anew. Let historical context appear only when a character's ongoing activity makes it matter. Follow established name pronunciation, character appearance, relationships, time and geography.

Do not repeat existing narration, add academic framing, invent historical dialogue, break into topic chapters or invent consequential incidents. Return naturally from earlier events to the action we left.

Provide spoken words only, in ElevenLabs-ready prose.
Additional directions: {{extra}}

Review the provided VENIVIDIVICI narration or outline for historical accuracy and continuity.
Subject: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Claim or scene focus: {{focus}}
Available source register: {{sources}}
Continuity to check against: {{continuity}}

For each substantial historical claim, identify whether it is (A) securely attested, (B) a disputed ancient account, (C) modern scholarly interpretation, (D) plausible atmospheric reconstruction or (E) invented minor-character perspective. Flag anachronistic buildings, titles, costumes, language, dates, geography and relationships.

Verify direct quotations against the actual ancient passage and distinguish translation from paraphrase; never invent citations or claim sources were consulted when they were not. Identify any fictional minor character improperly given privileged information or an important historical intervention.

Give a prioritized correction table with the exact wording to reconsider, what is wrong or uncertain, evidence needed and a historically responsible rewrite. Preserve the story's immersive narrative method. Do not automatically turn the narration into an academic lecture.
Additional checks: {{extra}}

Create exactly ONE separate 16:9 landscape illustration for VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind.

Date and setting: {{year}}, {{location}}
Main person: {{emperor}}
Action to depict: {{focus}}
Other figures: {{people}}
Atmosphere and lighting: {{mood}}
Approved visual references / character identity: {{visual}}

Match the established painterly visual identity: sophisticated Roman historical painting, fresco influence, refined reconstruction illustration, naturalistic painted faces, fine visible texture, historically plausible anatomy and materials, muted gold, ochre, terracotta, marble ivory, deep red, earthy greens and subdued blue.

Maintain strict date-specific accuracy for Roman clothing, hairstyles, buildings, furnishings, weapons and daily objects. Preserve likenesses from the supplied reference images; references set the visual identity, not necessarily the composition. Compose a single coherent full-frame scene.

NO glossy CGI, photorealistic film still, fantasy, Hollywood costume, modern object, later Roman monument not yet built, collage, split panels, watermarks, logos, captions, readable words or border. Avoid invented specifics presented as known facts.

Further details: {{extra}}

Develop a chronological visual production plan for the attached VENIVIDIVICI narration.
Episode subject: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Narration section: {{section}}
Images requested: {{length}}
Primary setting: {{location}}
Approved art / recurring figure reference: {{visual}}

Read the full narration and divide it into meaningful visual beats. Use atmospheric painted historical reconstructions rather than literal illustration of every sentence. For each image specify a unique code, approximate narration placement, scene description, 16:9 standalone generation prompt and any historical/character continuity constraints.

Match the established traditional history-painting visual style with authentic first-century Roman materials as appropriate to the date. Maintain consistent recurring appearances, clothing and architecture, and avoid all later monuments, modern features, watermarks and text.

Group the results in batches of ten. Reused frames, if useful, must be clearly identified rather than unnecessarily regenerated. Separate factual reconstruction notes from image prompts. Do not substitute your own narration.
Other production requirements: {{extra}}

Write a concise image-to-video animation prompt for an existing VENIVIDIVICI historical illustration.

Subject: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Setting: {{location}}
Scene activity: {{focus}}
Atmosphere: {{mood}}
Image / character reference: {{visual}}
Shot duration: {{length}}

Preserve the original image's layout, facial features, architecture, painting style and historical period. Animate only plausible gentle environment motion: very slow camera push or lateral pan, slight parallax, drifting smoke, flickering oil flame, shifting cloth, subtle water, soft clouds or natural light changes where relevant.

Avoid camera shake, rapid edits, photorealistic conversion, invented objects, altered faces, extreme character movement, modern effects and dramatic exaggeration. Produce one cohesive motion prompt, including explicit negative constraints.
Additional details: {{extra}}

Prepare publication materials for a completed episode of VENIVIDIVICI — Voices History Left Behind.

Historical subject: {{emperor}}
Period: {{year}}
Main narrative: {{focus}}
Final duration: {{length}}
Verified sources used: {{sources}}

Provide (1) five accurate YouTube title options suitable for this historically researched documentary series; (2) a concise spoiler-light synopsis and longer description; (3) a properly checked source list from sources actually consulted; (4) restrained, non-clickbait chapter suggestions if appropriate; (5) credits and necessary AI-use and rights disclosures; (6) relevant but selective search tags.

Preserve the dignity, immersive character-led tone and avoid unsupported sensational claims. Do not invent exact citations, production credits, video links, or other publication facts. Flag missing information explicitly.
Additional direction: {{extra}}
